==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*sekʷ-|id=follow||*pro-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|prōsecūtus}}, perfect participle of {{m|la|prōsequor}}. {{doublet|en|pursue}}, from Old French. Compare also {{m|en|persecute}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpɹɒsɪkjuːt/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-prosecute.wav|Audio (UK)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|legal}} To [[start]] [[criminal]] [[proceeding]]s against.
#: ''to '''prosecute''' a man for trespass, or for a riot''
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|Samson Agonistes|56|line=898|passage=To acquit themſelves and '''proſecute''' their foes}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|legal}} To [[charge]], [[try]].
#* '''1959''', {{w|William S. Burroughs}}, ''{{w|Naked Lunch}}'', page 9
#*: The Vigilante is '''prosecuted''' in Federal Court under a lynch bill and winds up in a Federal Nut House specially designed for the containment of ghosts {{...}}
# To seek to obtain by legal process.
#: ''to '''prosecute''' a right or a claim in a court of law''
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[pursue]] something to the [[end]].
#: ''to '''prosecute''' a scheme, hope, or claim''
#*: {{RQ:Shakespeare Midsummer|I|i|passage=I am beloved of beauteous Hermia; / Why should not I, then, '''prosecute''' my right?}}

